<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nearchus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 360 – c. 300 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nearchus was a Cretan-born officer and boyhood friend of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>. In 325 and 324 BCE he commanded the fleet that sailed from the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a> along the coasts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabian-sea">Arabian Sea</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, while Alexander marched through the Gedrosian desert. His account of the voyage was used by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arrian">Arrian</a> in his Indica.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Nearchus was a native of Lato in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a> and the son of Androtimus. His family settled at Amphipolis in Macedonia during the reign of Philip II, when Nearchus was probably a young boy. He was almost certainly older than Alexander, like Ptolemy, Erigyius and the other boyhood friends. With Ptolemy, Erigyius, Laomedon and Harpalus he was one of Alexander's mentors, and Philip exiled him over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pixodarus">Pixodarus</a> affair. The exiles were recalled on Alexander's accession.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Nearchus was appointed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrap</a> of Lycia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamphylia">Pamphylia</a> in 334/3 BCE, one of Alexander's earliest satrapal appointments. His naval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a> of Persian fleets threatening the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> helped Alexander's conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenicia">Phoenicia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonia">Babylonia</a>. In 328 BCE he rejoined Alexander in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a> with reinforcements, and after the siege of Aornos he led a reconnaissance mission, especially to learn about elephants.</p><p>In 326 BCE he was made admiral of the fleet built on the Hydaspes. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trierarchy">trierarchy</a> was a financial responsibility: he put up the money for the boats. At Pattala he prepared to lead 17–20,000 men into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a> while Alexander crossed the Gedrosian desert. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monsoon">Monsoon</a> rains delayed the fleet for 24 days near the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a>. It then passed Morontobara and the coast of the Fish-Eaters of Makran, and reached Carmania in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, where it met Alexander. It came to the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a> in 324 BCE.</p><p>At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susa">Susa</a> weddings Nearchus married the daughter of Barsine and Mentor. He was to command the fleet for Alexander's planned conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>, but the king's death ended the plan. He then supported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heracles">Heracles</a>, Alexander's son by Barsine, and later joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigonus-i-monophthalmus">Antigonus</a>. His last mention is as an adviser to Demetrius in 313/2 BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Nearchus wrote a history of his voyage together with a description of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, the Indike. It is lost, but its contents are known from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a> and other later authors and from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arrian">Arrian</a>'s Indica of the 2nd century CE. It likely had two parts: one on India's frontiers, population, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">castes</a>, fauna, flora, cultures and armies, and one on the voyage towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>. According to Arrian, he described how <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugarcane">sugarcane</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> fabrics were grown, made and traded in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus-valley">Indus Valley</a>. He reputedly was the first Greek commander to visit <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahrain">Bahrain</a>, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tylos">Tylos</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nearchus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Nearchus</b>, Alexander the Great's admiral, came from Lato.</blockquote><a href="/state/lato">Lato</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Lato, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=35.18,25.66&amp;year=-321" aria-label="Lato on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5200 892 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Nearchus"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5650 1162h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5650 1162h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=35.18,25.66&amp;year=-321">What was here in 321 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
